-- Foundation type-constructor helpers — parametric ADTs and opaque-wrapped atoms.
--
-- The parametric helpers (Maybe, Result, Either) are the spine of every
-- typed function signature in Fenel: Maybe for absence, Result for fallible
-- operations, Either for biased sums without failure semantics. Fields are
-- positional (auto-named `_0`) so `Just x` / `Err e` / `Ok a` bind by ORDER
-- per Phase 10a-6's positional-constructor-binding contract. Named-record
-- form `Just { _0 = x }` still parses (per the universal named-args
-- convention), but it produces a different wire shape (`{value: x}` style is
-- gone for std-lib ADTs).
--
-- The opaque-wrapped atoms (Bool, Nat, Int) carry a pure ADT view (the
-- canonical mathematical / type-theoretic definition). The type system reasons
-- about the pure form (so `Maybe Bool`, `case b of True -> … | False -> …`,
-- etc. compose uniformly with every other ADT); the runtime stores the native
-- shape (JS booleans / numbers).
--
-- representation-substrate Phase 5a (2026-06-25) — the decorative `repr` STRING
-- field RETIRED from the `$opaque` TYPE. The native runtime rep now lives in the
-- per-type `Reprable JsRuntime` instance (`fenel/std/dispatch-instances.fenel`)
-- — the precise per-type lift/lower, not a coarse string (Nat & Int were BOTH
-- "number"). The surface syntax `opaque "<repr>" Name = …` below KEEPS the string
-- token as a now-IGNORED annotation (the loader reads it only as the "is-opaque"
-- flag).
--
-- Phase 5b-final (2026-06-25) — the `$opaque { name, pure }` WRAPPER is KEPT: it
-- is the NOMINAL-NEWTYPE marker (`name` = nominal identity, `pure` = the ADT
-- definition). Step-0 proved full removal collapses `String` into `List Char`
-- (byte-identical once both wrappers are stripped; the sole distinguisher is
-- `$opaque.name`) — R2 option (ii); see
-- `workstreams/representation-substrate/notes/phase-5b-step0-probe.md`. A
-- `$opaque`→`$nominal` rename (now that `repr` is gone) is deferred to its own
-- phase (a cross-cutting + persisted-data sweep).
--
-- This file is the canonical Fenel-syntax definition (the spec). The TS
-- helpers in `implementations/typescript/engine/src/type-foundation.ts`
-- (`Maybe(...)` / `Result(...)` / `Either(...)` / `List(...)`) parse this
-- file at runtime and substitute the parametric type-vars to derive each
-- call's result. The non-parametric atoms (Bool, Nat, Int) keep TS inline
-- literals as the production source, with `foundation-load.test.ts`
-- asserting the two paths agree.

-- FM-retire-E1 Step 7 — the foundation parametric ADTs carry their catalog
-- identity as directives (lifted to Codeable / Labelable / Describable /
-- Iconable by the loader, auto-registered by system-entities.ts). Their inline
-- `defineSystemEntity` calls are DELETED. Facet text is byte-identical to the
-- pre-flip TS literals (the oracle locks it).

@entity
@label "Maybe"
@icon "help-circle"
@description "Optional value — Nothing | Just a. The canonical encoding of absence in Fenel; no nulls anywhere."
Maybe a = Nothing | Just a

@entity
@label "Result"
@icon "split"
@description "Tagged union of success/failure — Err e | Ok a. Use for any expression whose evaluation can fail with a typed error."
Result e a = Err e | Ok a

@entity
@label "Either"
@icon "split"
@description "Haskell-style biased sum — Left a | Right b. Use for value-level Or-shapes without failure semantics. Draft<T>'s field wrappers are Either<String, T_field>: Left = raw input awaiting reconciliation, Right = parsed-and-validated typed value."
Either a b = Left a | Right b

-- Atoms — opaque-wrapped ADTs.

@entity
@codeName "Boolean"
opaque "boolean" Bool = True | False

-- representation-substrate Phase 2 — the identity directives REGISTER `Nat` as a
-- catalog entity (the Maybe/Result/Either auto-registration path:
-- `foundationEntityRegs` only registers a decl that lifts a `Codeable`, i.e. one
-- carrying an identity directive). Registering Nat is what lets the live
-- registry `$opaque`-peel (registry.ts) index its `Zero`/`Succ` cases into
-- `_constructors` — DISSOLVING the construct gap (positional `Succ x` now builds
-- correctly; the numeric-tower Phase-1 named-field workaround retires). Nat's
-- pure is a direct `$variant`, so the Phase-1 peel handles it with no extension.
@entity
@label "Natural number"
@icon "hash"
@description "Peano natural number — Zero | Succ pred. The non-negative integers built inductively; opaque-wrapped over a JS number for runtime performance, the pure ADT for reasoning."
opaque "number" Nat = Zero | Succ pred: Nat

-- numeric-tower Phase 2 — the identity directives REGISTER `Int` as a catalog
-- entity (the same Maybe/Result/Either/Nat auto-registration path:
-- `foundationEntityRegs` registers a decl that lifts a `Codeable`, i.e. one
-- carrying an identity directive). Registering Int makes it browsable like
-- Nat/Bool and is the principled `Reprable JsRuntime Int` home; it also indexes
-- `_constructors.Int = ["magnitude", "negative"]` via the live registry
-- `$opaque`-peel so the NAMED-record surface (`Int { magnitude = m, negative =
-- n }`) the Int arithmetic bodies use is unambiguous. Int's pure is a single-case
-- `$record`, so the Phase-1 peel handles it with no extension (Nat precedent).
@entity
@label "Integer"
@icon "hash"
@description "Signed integer in sign-magnitude form — Int magnitude: Nat negative: Bool. The whole numbers (positive, negative, zero); opaque-wrapped over a JS number for runtime performance, the pure ADT for reasoning. Negative zero is canonicalized away by the mkInt smart constructor (numeric-tower Phase 2)."
opaque "number" Int = Int magnitude: Nat negative: Bool

-- Parametric recursive opaque atom — the canonical list. The `tail: List a`
-- self-reference parses to `{$named: "List"}` (Phase 2.5 parser fix),
-- matching the shape the TS canonical literal emits.
--
-- representation-substrate Phase 3 — the identity directives REGISTER `List` as
-- a catalog entity (the same Maybe/Result/Either/Nat/Int/Rational
-- auto-registration path: `foundationEntityRegs` registers a decl carrying an
-- identity directive, gated on the lifted Codeable). Registering List makes it
-- browsable like Nat/Int/Bool, is the principled `Reprable JsRuntime (List a)`
-- home, and — the construct-gap dissolution — indexes `_constructors.Nil = []` /
-- `_constructors.Cons = ["head", "tail"]` via the live registry `$opaque`-peel,
-- so the bridge bodies' positional `Cons h t` / `Nil` build/match the canonical
-- `{$tag:"Cons", _0:…, _1:…}` / `{$tag:"Nil"}` surface (the Nat `Succ x`
-- precedent — List's pure is a direct `$variant`, so the Phase-1 peel handles it
-- with no extension). Directives do not change the `$opaque` shape, so
-- foundation-load stays byte-identical.
@entity
@label "List"
@icon "list"
@description "The canonical homogeneous list — Nil | Cons head: a tail: List a. The free monoid over a (μX. 1 + a × X); opaque-wrapped over a JS array for runtime performance, the pure cons-spine ADT for reasoning. The Reprable JsRuntime (List a) instance bridges the cons-spine ↔ array, carrying each element through untouched (element-transparent — the element's own Reprable composes on top)."
opaque "array" List a = Nil | Cons head: a tail: List a

-- Char — Unicode scalar codepoint. Modeled as a refinement of Nat over the
-- Unicode codespace ([0..0x10FFFF]) minus the UTF-16 surrogate range
-- (0xD800..0xDFFF, not valid scalars). Phase 2.6 introduces the
-- `value ∈ <set>` refinement syntax that lowers to a raw SetExpr (no $where
-- wrap) so set-algebra can decide subset/disjoint structurally rather than
-- evaluating a predicate value-by-value. Phase 2.7 extends `parseSetExpr`
-- with `0x` hex literals so the codepoint bounds read naturally.
--
-- representation-substrate Phase 4 — the identity directives REGISTER `Char` as
-- a catalog entity (the Maybe/Nat/Int/List/String `foundationEntityRegs` path:
-- a decl carrying an identity directive lifts a `Codeable`). Registering Char
-- makes it browsable like the rest of the tower and is the principled `Reprable
-- JsRuntime Char` home. UNLIKE List/Nat, Char's pure is a *refined Nat* (NOT a
-- `$variant`), so this registers Char for home/browsability ONLY — it has no own
-- constructors, so the `$opaque`-peel indexes NO `_constructors` (the String
-- "register-for-home-only" precedent). Directives do not change the `$opaque`
-- shape, so foundation-load stays byte-identical.
@entity
@label "Character"
@icon "type"
@description "A Unicode scalar codepoint — opaque-wrapped over a 1-character JS string for runtime performance (the `codepoint` repr), the pure form a refined Nat over the Unicode codespace [0..0x10FFFF] minus the UTF-16 surrogate range. The Reprable JsRuntime Char instance bridges the 1-char-string native ↔ the codepoint Number via the charFromCode/charCode host leaves (number-direct — the refined Nat surfaces as its base number repr, not a materialized Peano spine)."
opaque "codepoint" Char = Nat | value ∈ [0..0xD7FF] ∪ [0xE000..0x10FFFF]

-- Phase 2.7 atoms.
--
-- PositiveNat and NonNegInt are pure refinement aliases — direct
-- applications of the Phase 2.6 SetExpr-bearing refinement syntax.
-- Rational is the compound case: an opaque-wrapped record whose `den`
-- field references the named refinement, exercising the parser's
-- field-position type slot against a refinement-aliased atom. The TS
-- helpers in `type-foundation.ts` (`PositiveNat` / `NonNegInt` /
-- `Rational`) carry inline literals whose shape matches what these
-- decls parse to; `foundation-load.test.ts` asserts the two paths
-- agree byte-for-byte.

PositiveNat = Nat | value ∈ [1..)

NonNegInt = Int | value ∈ [0..)

-- containment-substrate Phase 3 — the inductive↔refinement iso codomain
-- aliases for the Int / Rational / Real tower rungs (the NonNegInt precedent,
-- one rung up). Each is the REFINEMENT presentation of a tower rung: the
-- numeric tower's D8 iso witnesses (int/rat/real ToRefined) were stubbed with
-- codomain `Number` because no refinement alias was registered (see the
-- *-bridge.fenel comment ladders); these decls close that gap, mirroring how
-- `natToRefined : Nat -> NonNegInt` already names ℕ's refinement presentation.
--
-- The defining predicate is `value ∈ <glyph>` where ℤ/ℚ/ℝ are the registered
-- numeric named-sets (system-entities.ts: ℤ→membershipInt, ℚ/ℝ→membershipReal)
-- — the SAME glyphs the NAMED_HIERARCHY rows use, so the alias ties the
-- refinement view to the existing membership decider (no `isInteger` Fenel fn
-- needed; the named-set IS the decider). Base is `Number` (the ambient), per
-- the tower audit's D8 spec `{ n: Number | isInteger n }` / `{ q: Number |
-- isRational q }` / `{ x: Number | isReal x }`. ℝ's membershipReal accepts the
-- finite reals, so RealNumber is a (mild) proper refinement of Number, not a
-- bare identity — recorded honestly. The TS twins in `type-foundation.ts`
-- (Integer / RationalNumber / RealNumber) carry the matching `$tag SeRef`
-- inline literals; `foundation-load.test.ts` asserts byte-parity.

Integer = Int | value ∈ ℤ

RationalNumber = Rational | value ∈ ℚ

RealNumber = Number | value ∈ ℝ

-- numeric-tower Phase 3 — the identity directives REGISTER `Rational` as a
-- catalog entity (the Maybe/Result/Either/Nat/Int auto-registration path:
-- `foundationEntityRegs` registers a decl carrying an identity directive).
-- Registering Rational makes it browsable like Nat/Int/Bool, is the principled
-- `Reprable JsRuntime Rational` home, and indexes `_constructors.Rational =
-- ["num", "den"]` via the live registry `$opaque`-peel so the NAMED-record
-- surface (`Rational { num = q, den = d }`) the Rational arithmetic bodies use
-- is unambiguous. Rational is the FIRST rung with a COMPOUND pure form (a
-- single-case `$record` of two lower rungs — `num: Int`, `den: PositiveNat`),
-- but it is still a single-case `$record`, so the Phase-1 `$opaque`-peel handles
-- it with no extension (the Nat/Int precedent). Directives do not change the
-- `$opaque` shape, so foundation-load stays byte-identical.
@entity
@label "Rational"
@icon "divide"
@description "Rational number in lowest terms — Rational num: Int den: PositiveNat. The fractions ℚ; opaque-wrapped over a JS number for runtime performance (a LOSSY perf branch — 1/3 cannot round-trip through a double), the pure ratio ADT for reasoning. Kept canonical (lowest terms, positive denominator, 0 = 0/1) by the mkRational smart constructor (numeric-tower Phase 3)."
opaque "rational" Rational = Rational num: Int den: PositiveNat

-- numeric-tower Phase 4 — the convergence-proof STUB type for Real's `converges`
-- field. A real (Cauchy-with-modulus) carries a dependent proof that its modulus
-- genuinely witnesses Cauchy-ness (∀k, ∀ m n ≥ modulus k, |seq m − seq n| ≤
-- 1/2^k). The PRINCIPLED form of that field is `$el { call: [realConverges, seq,
-- modulus] }` — a dependent proposition whose inhabitant IS the proof
-- ([[feedback_laws_supers_are_el_dependent_typing]]). But the `$el` dependent-type
-- lift is DISABLED inside constructor fields (design/syntax-reference.md — a
-- lowercase-applied head in a positional/named ctor field stays type-vars, never
-- `$el`), so the parser CANNOT author an `$el`-keyed field here. Per the brief §0/
-- §2c + the D8c iso-stub precedent, `converges` is a PARSER-AUTHORABLE typed stub:
-- a nullary proposition `Cauchy` whose single witness `CauchyAx` is the
-- Axiom-discharged proof. The HEADLINE holds — the field EXISTS and is
-- proof-typed (an inhabitant of a proposition), carrying dependent-types-ship-
-- today. TIGHTENING it to the kernel-checked `$el { call: [...] }` predicate (which
-- would ride the ledgered B3 `$`-key marshal at the loader, NOT a new TS kind) is a
-- DEFERRED follow-up (numeric-tower Phase 4 fence §3 — no kernel proof this phase).
@entity
@label "Cauchy convergence"
@icon "check-circle"
@description "Convergence-proof witness for a Real (Cauchy-sequence-with-modulus). The single nullary witness CauchyAx is the Axiom-discharged proof that a real's modulus witnesses Cauchy-ness; a typed STUB this phase (the parser cannot author the principled `$el { call: [realConverges, seq, modulus] }` field inside a constructor — $el is disabled in ctor fields). The field EXISTS + is proof-typed; tightening to a kernel obligation is deferred (numeric-tower Phase 4)."
Cauchy = CauchyAx

-- numeric-tower Phase 4 — `Real` = the LAST tower rung, the type that does not
-- exist at HEAD (only a SUBTYPE_LATTICE target + the membershipReal decider
-- reference it). D3: a real is a Cauchy-sequence-WITH-MODULUS — a Σ-type
--   Real seq: (Nat -> Rational) modulus: (Nat -> Nat) converges: Cauchy
-- where `seq n` is the n-th rational approximation, `modulus k` is the index N
-- past which all terms are within 1/2^k (the MODULUS CONVENTION — `1/2^k`, chosen
-- at Step 0; it is what makes the real CONSTRUCTIVE, telling a consumer how far to
-- sample for a requested precision), and `converges` is the (stubbed) proof that
-- the modulus witnesses Cauchy-ness. UNLIKE the finite rungs (Nat/Int/Rational),
-- a real is an INFINITE object — there is NO finite canonical form, so this rung
-- BREAKS the smart-constructor inheritance: there is no `mkReal` that
-- canonicalizes; the modulus + convergence proof carry the well-formedness the
-- normalizer carried below. Real arithmetic (realAdd/realSub/realMul/realNegate,
-- real-arith.fenel) stays TOTAL (constructive, pointwise over the Phase-3 Rational
-- ops + Nat modulus bookkeeping); only COMPARISON goes PARTIAL — realLt/realEq
-- return `Maybe Bool` (D4), the FIRST typed-partial ops in the tower (the `Nothing`
-- IS the decidability boundary as a value). The fields are FUNCTION-typed (the
-- arrow `Nat -> Rational` / `Nat -> Nat` field types parse to `$arrow` and a Real
-- value carries lambda closures in those slots — proven at Step 0). The native rep
-- is `Number` (a double is a SAMPLE of the sequence at one precision — far lossier
-- than Rational; the D7 ℝ carve-out made total), via the Reprable JsRuntime/Jsonb
-- Real instances. opaque "real" labels the rung; the runtime value is the
-- function-record (no JS-native real exists).
@entity
@label "Real"
@icon "infinity"
@description "Real number — Cauchy-sequence-with-modulus (D3): Real seq: (Nat -> Rational) modulus: (Nat -> Nat) converges: Cauchy. The reals ℝ; the LAST tower rung. seq is the rational approximating sequence, modulus the modulus of convergence (modulus k ⇒ all terms within 1/2^k — the constructive content), converges the (stubbed) proof the modulus witnesses Cauchy-ness. Arithmetic is TOTAL (pointwise over Rational); equality/order go PARTIAL → Maybe Bool (real equality is decidably-undecidable, the decidability boundary as a typed value). No finite canonical form — a real is an infinite object; opaque-wrapped over a Number sample for runtime performance (LOSSIER than Rational — a sample at one precision, the D7 ℝ carve-out)."
opaque "real" Real = Real seq: (Nat -> Rational) modulus: (Nat -> Nat) converges: Cauchy

-- numeric-tower Phase 5 (2026-06-26) — `Number` = the TAGGED `$variant` over the
-- tower (D2, THE CAPSTONE FLIP). At HEAD `Number` was a bare LEAF name with NO
-- registered FenelType (resolveNamed("Number") → undefined) — a double pretending
-- to be ℕ/ℤ/ℚ/ℝ at once. This decl makes it HONEST: an opaque-wrapped tagged sum
-- `NatN Nat | IntN Int | RatN Rational | RealN Real`, the `$opaque{repr,pure}`
-- pattern applied to Number ITSELF — the pure tagged union is the SOURCE OF TRUTH,
-- the `"number"` double repr is the cross-rung PERFORMANCE branch (the flat
-- `@native` arith/comparison + the DirectOp F64 lowering STAY, D1/D7; they are the
-- IEEE fast path). [[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]
--
-- (FORK 2 — the Number-tag-vs-double-repr, the genuine Rom-call, RESOLVED the
-- brief's lean.) A JS double ERASES which rung a value belongs to, so the tag is
-- LOSSY across the repr boundary. The reconciliation (number-tower.fenel): `lift`
-- collapses a tagged value to its double (NatN x ↦ natToNumber x, …); `lower` tags
-- a double — INTEGRAL ⇒ IntN (numberToInt d), else RealN (numberToReal d) — the
-- same lossy-but-total carve-out as Real's Reprable (the tag is RECOVERED by
-- inspection, never round-tripped exactly). NatN/RatN are constructible + dispatch
-- targets but `lower` never PRODUCES them (no finite test distinguishes ℕ⊂ℤ or a
-- non-integer double's exact rung) — they are the honest "the double lost it".
--
-- THE P7-1 ISO (set-algebra-to-fenel B§5) — this is a TAGGED `$variant` (each rung
-- injected under a tag that forces disjointness even where ℕ⊂ℤ⊂ℚ⊂ℝ overlap),
-- which is EXACTLY `$variant`. So the tower validates `$variant ≅
-- tagged-disjoint-$union` (P7-1) and does NOT trigger the deferred tier-2 untagged
-- `$union` former. Number STAYS finite-membership (membershipReal/NAMED_HIERARCHY
-- decide it by NAME, unchanged) + inline-typeable (TYPEABLE_SCALAR_TYPES).
@entity
@label "Number"
@icon "hash"
@description "The numeric tower as one type — Number = NatN Nat | IntN Int | RatN Rational | RealN Real (numeric-tower Phase 5, D2). A TAGGED $variant (P7-1: $variant ≅ tagged-disjoint-$union — the rungs are disjoint under their tags even where ℕ⊂ℤ⊂ℚ⊂ℝ overlap), opaque-wrapped over a JS double (the `number` repr) for runtime performance: the pure tagged union is the source of truth, the double the cross-rung perf branch (the flat @native arith + DirectOp F64 lowering, the IEEE fast path). The Reprable JsRuntime/Jsonb Number lift collapses a tagged value to its double; lower tags a double — integral ⇒ IntN, else RealN (the lossy-but-total FORK-2 carve-out). The Numeric/Ord/Eq classes dispatch arithmetic + comparison per rung."
opaque "number" Number = NatN Nat | IntN Int | RatN Rational | RealN Real

-- String — opaque, backed by JS string for runtime performance.
-- Pure equivalent: List Char (μX. 1 + Char × X). The pure form lets the
-- type system reason about strings as character lists uniformly with every
-- other list ADT; the JS-string repr is the runtime fast path. Phase 2.11
-- introduces the opaque-decl type-expr-RHS grammar specifically for this
-- migration — `List Char` is a parametric type-application, not a
-- constructor list, so the prior `opaque "<repr>" Name = Ctor1 | …` /
-- `opaque "<repr>" Name = Ctor field: T …` shapes don't fit. The new third
-- arm admits a bare type expression on the RHS (no `|`, no `name:` field
-- marker at depth 0 — see parseOpaqueDecl + opaqueBodyIsTypeExpr).
--
-- representation-substrate Phase 3 — the identity directives REGISTER `String`
-- as a catalog entity (the List/Nat/Int/Rational auto-registration path).
-- Registration is for browsability + the principled `Reprable JsRuntime String`
-- home, NOT for `_constructors`: String has no own constructors (`List Char` is
-- a type-application, so the `$opaque`-peel finds nothing to index — the Int
-- "registration-not-strictly-required-but-right" note). String's native is a JS
-- string (distinct from List's array — R4 per-(target,type) granularity), so its
-- Reprable instance is its OWN, not List's at `a = Char`. Directives do not
-- change the `$opaque` shape, so foundation-load stays byte-identical.
@entity
@label "String"
@icon "type"
@description "Text — opaque over a JS string for runtime performance; pure equivalent List Char (μX. 1 + Char × X), so the type system reasons about strings as character lists uniformly with every other list ADT. The Reprable JsRuntime String instance bridges the JS string ↔ List Char via chars/join (the Char element repr is a 1-char string); contrast List's element-transparent spine bridge — String's native is a flat code-point sequence, so its lift/lower split/join."
opaque "string" String = List Char

@entity
@label "Byte"
@icon "binary"
@description "An unsigned byte: a natural number from 0 through 255."
Byte = Nat | value ∈ [0..255]

@entity
@label "Bytes"
@icon "binary"
@description "A finite sequence of unsigned bytes. The pure view is List Byte; the host carrier is a Uint8Array."
opaque "bytes" Bytes = List Byte
